# Matrix Player — direct USB access to the DAC.
#
# WHY THIS IS NEEDED
# Matrix Player's primary output talks to the DAC over libusb, bypassing ALSA
# entirely. libusb opens /dev/bus/usb/<bus>/<dev> read-write, but the kernel
# creates those nodes root:root 0664 — a normal user gets read only, so the
# open fails with EACCES and the USB backend can't start. Nothing about the
# app can fix that from userspace; the permission has to be granted here.
#
# You do NOT need to blacklist snd-usb-audio or otherwise pry the device away
# from ALSA. The driver calls libusb_set_auto_detach_kernel_driver() and
# detaches the audio-control/streaming interfaces itself at open time, so the
# kernel driver steps aside on its own and reattaches when the app exits.
#
# INSTALL (one time, needs root once — after this, never sudo to play)
#   sudo install -m 0644 scripts/linux/70-matrix-player-usb.rules \
#        /etc/udev/rules.d/70-matrix-player-usb.rules
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#   sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=usb --action=add
# Or just replug the DAC after reloading. Verify with:
#   getfacl /dev/bus/usb/$(lsusb | sed -n 's/Bus \([0-9]*\) Device \([0-9]*\).*32bb:0004.*/\1\/\2/p')
# and look for a "user:<you>:rw-" line.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" hands the decision to systemd-logind: it puts an ACL on the
# device node for whoever is currently logged in at the local seat, and revokes
# it at logout. That is the reason this file needs no GROUP= and no group
# membership, and why it keeps working across user switches. Must be numbered
# below 73 so the tag is set before 73-seat-late.rules acts on it.
#
# ADDING ANOTHER DAC
# One line per device. The app itself takes any VID/PID (Audio Settings, stored
# as usb_vid/usb_pid), so this file is the only place a new DAC has to be
# named. Find the ID with `lsusb`; the hex must be lowercase here.

# HiBy FC4 — UAC2, High-Speed
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", ATTR{idVendor}=="32bb", ATTR{idProduct}=="0004", TAG+="uaccess"

# Example — copy this line, fill in your own IDs, uncomment:
#SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", ATTR{idVendor}=="xxxx", ATTR{idProduct}=="yyyy", TAG+="uaccess"

# FALLBACK for systems without systemd-logind (no uaccess support): grant the
# device to a group instead and add yourself to it (`usermod -aG audio $USER`,
# then log out and back in). Prefer uaccess above where it is available — it
# needs no group and follows the active session.
#SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", ATTR{idVendor}=="32bb", ATTR{idProduct}=="0004", MODE="0660", GROUP="audio"
